    topic: "A marathon preparation talk",
    audioText: "Good evening, everybody, and welcome to the final information meeting for our club's marathon group. My name is Aziz, and I have been coaching runners for eleven years. The race takes place on the fourteenth of October, which is exactly twelve weeks from today, and tonight I want to explain the training plan and the mistakes I see every year.\n\nThe plan is simple. You will run four times a week, and one of those runs is a long one on Sunday morning. We start with ten kilometres and add a little each week, but never more than ten percent. Last year a man in our group added too much too quickly and had to stop with a sore knee in week six. The longest run will be thirty two kilometres, three weeks before the race. Some people ask why we do not run the full forty two kilometres in training. The answer is that your body needs time to recover, and the damage from such a long run would cost you more than it gives you.\n\nNow, a change from what I told some of you in the spring. At that time I recommended eating huge plates of pasta the night before the race. I have changed my mind. Research suggests that you should increase your carbohydrates over the last three days, not just in one meal, and you should avoid anything new or heavy. On race morning, have breakfast three hours before the start. The race begins at half past seven, so that means your alarm should ring at four thirty, which I know sounds terrible.\n\nLet me talk about equipment. Buy your shoes at least a month before the race and wear them in training. The worst thing you can do is to put on brand new shoes at the start line, because you will almost certainly get blisters. The same applies to clothes. Do not try a new shirt on race day.\n\nOn pace, beginners usually start far too fast because of the crowd and the excitement. Please run the first five kilometres slower than your target speed. If your goal is five hours, which is realistic for most of you, you should average about seven minutes per kilometre. If you feel tired, there is no shame in walking for one minute at each water station. The only people who must not walk are those aiming for under four hours, but I do not think anyone in this room is in that group.\n\nFinally, the last week. Run only twice, very gently, and take Friday as a complete rest day. Sleep is more important than any workout now. Questions? Yes, the lady in the green jacket, please.",
