    topic: "A dentist appointment",
    audioText: "Kamila has been putting off this appointment for almost a year, and as she sits in the waiting room she wishes she had never made it. Eventually the dentist, Doctor Evans, invites her in and asks what the problem is. Kamila explains that she has had a pain in a tooth at the bottom on the left side for about three weeks. It hurts mainly when she drinks something cold, but yesterday it also hurt when she had hot tea. At first she thought it would pass, which she now admits was a mistake.\n\nDoctor Evans examines the tooth and takes an X-ray. After a few minutes he explains that there is a large hole, a cavity, that has reached quite deep into the tooth. The good news is that he can save it. Kamila had been afraid that it would have to be taken out, but a filling will be enough, and he does not think a root treatment is necessary, although he says that if she had waited another month, it might have been. Kamila feels a mixture of relief and embarrassment.\n\nThe dentist then talks about the procedure. He will give her an injection to numb the area, which will last about two hours, so she should not eat or drink anything hot until it wears off, otherwise she might burn her tongue without noticing. The filling itself takes about twenty minutes. Kamila asks whether it will hurt, and he promises that she will feel only a little pressure. She says she has always been terrified of the sound of the drill, and he kindly offers her headphones with music, which she gratefully accepts.\n\nWhile he works, Doctor Evans chats about her teeth in general. He notices that her gums are slightly red and asks how often she cleans between her teeth. Kamila says she brushes twice a day but never uses floss. He says that is the cause of the redness, and that without change she might develop gum disease in a few years. He recommends floss or small brushes once a day. Kamila asks whether an electric toothbrush would be better. He says it is helpful but not necessary, and that technique matters more than the price of the brush.\n\nAt the end, the receptionist tells her that the treatment costs one hundred and fifty dollars, which is covered partly by her insurance, so she pays only sixty. Doctor Evans asks her to return in six months for a check, not in a year, as she did this time. Kamila promises that she will, and she leaves feeling much braver than she expected, even though her mouth feels strange and her lip is still numb.",
