    topic: "A lecture on stress",
    audioText: "Good morning, everyone. Today's lecture is about stress, a word we all use but rarely define. Let me begin with a surprise. Stress is not always bad. The scientist Hans Selye, who first described it in the nineteen thirties, argued that a little stress helps us perform. Before an exam or a job interview, your heart beats faster and your attention sharpens. That is acute stress, and it usually disappears when the situation ends. The real danger is chronic stress, which continues for weeks and months, for example because of an unhappy job or financial worries.\n\nTo show how serious this can be, I want to describe a study that followed four hundred office workers for five years. The researchers found that those who reported constant pressure were nearly twice as likely to develop high blood pressure. I should add that the study had a weakness: most participants were men over forty, so we cannot be certain that the same results would apply to young women. A second finding was interesting. It was not the amount of work that caused the most harm, but the feeling of having no control. Workers who could decide how to organise their day suffered less, even if they worked longer hours.\n\nSo what can we do? I will mention three things. The first is physical activity. Even a brisk walk of twenty minutes lowers the level of stress hormones. The second is sleep. People who sleep badly react to small problems as if they were disasters. The third is breathing. A simple exercise is to breathe in for four seconds, hold for seven, and breathe out for eight. Some of my students laughed when I first suggested it, but those who tried it said it worked.\n\nNow I would like to express an opinion that not everyone in my field shares. Many apps promise to remove stress with five minute meditations. In my view they are oversold. I have no objection to them, and some people clearly benefit, but they cannot replace the deeper causes, such as a heavy workload or lack of social contact. Spending time with friends, in my experience, is far more effective than any app. I was sceptical about this myself, but the data are consistent.\n\nBefore the break, I should mention what stress is not. It is not a sign of weakness, and it is not something you can simply decide not to feel. After the break we will look at stress in students, and next week there will be a short test on today's material. Please read chapter six before then, and do not leave it until the last night, as many of you did last time.",
