    topic: "A yoga retreat booking call",
    audioText: "Dana phones the reception of the Silver Valley Yoga Retreat on a Sunday evening, and a cheerful receptionist named Leyla answers. Dana explains that she wants to book a week in the mountains because she has been working long hours and her doctor has advised her to rest. Leyla says that the retreat offers seven night programmes, starting every second Saturday from April until October.\n\nDana first asks about June. Leyla checks the calendar and says that the programme beginning on the seventh of June still has places. Dana hesitates, because her manager has not yet approved her holiday, and she says that the end of the summer would be better. After a short pause, she changes her mind again, saying that September would be quieter at work, and asks whether the programme on the twelfth of September is available. Leyla says that it is, but only two places are left, so Dana should decide quickly.\n\nThen they talk about accommodation. A single room costs nine hundred dollars for the week, and a shared room costs six hundred and fifty per person. Dana prefers privacy, but the price is high, so she asks for the shared room, and she would be happy to be matched with a quiet person. Leyla promises to check with the other guests and notes that the shared rooms have two beds and a small balcony with a view of the valley.\n\nDana is vegetarian and asks about the food. Leyla says that all meals are vegetarian, with the exception of Saturday dinner, when fish is served, but guests can always ask for an alternative. Breakfast is at half past seven, after the morning yoga session, which starts at six. There is no coffee, only herbal tea, and Dana laughs and says that she will manage, though she suspects the first two days will be hard. Phones are allowed in the rooms but not in the yoga hall.\n\nFinally, Dana asks about cancellation. Leyla explains the rules carefully. If Dana cancels more than thirty days before the start, she gets a full refund. Between thirty and fifteen days before, she gets fifty percent back, and if she cancels later than that, there is no refund at all. Dana says that this seems fair. To keep the room she must pay a deposit of two hundred dollars within three days. Dana says she will do it tomorrow morning by card, and Leyla sends her an email with all the details and the booking number. Dana thanks her and says she is already looking forward to the quiet.",
